{"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"tokens":[{"kind":"attribute","text":"@frozen"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"ShortestPathTree"},{"kind":"text","text":"<"},{"kind":"genericParameter","text":"G"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"kind":"genericParameter","text":"Distance"},{"kind":"text","text":"> "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"where"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"G"},{"kind":"text","text":" : "},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols08DirectedA0P","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedGraph","kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"DirectedGraph"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Distance"},{"kind":"text","text":" : "},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:s18AdditiveArithmeticP","kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"AdditiveArithmetic"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Distance"},{"kind":"text","text":" : "},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:SL","kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Comparable"}],"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"kind":"content","content":[{"level":2,"text":"Overview","anchor":"overview","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Ties.","type":"text"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" The first relaxation to a vertex’s final distance wins. So ","type":"text"},{"code":"parent(of: v)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"shortest-path predecessor of ","type":"text"},{"code":"v","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" settled first. Vertices at equal distance settle in an","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"unspecified order, so when several vertices at the same smallest distance lead to ","type":"text"},{"code":"v","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" along","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"shortest paths, which one is the parent is unspecified. Among parallel edges from the parent,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the parent edge is the cheapest, and the first in ","type":"text"},{"code":"outEdges(of:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" order among equals.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"A tree holds a copy of the graph, so looking a vertex up never hashes on a representation","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"with cheap vertex indices. While the tree is alive, the next mutation of the original copies","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"it; a tree of a graph about to change should be read and dropped first.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Several sources."}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" Each source starts at distance zero with no parent. Dijkstra’s algorithm","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"and breadth-first search never improve on that, so a source keeps no parent even when another","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"source reaches it. Bellman–Ford treats the sources as one super-source (NetworkX’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"_bellman_ford","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"): a negative path from one source to another improves the second, which then","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"has that distance, a parent and a path from the first.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Not ","type":"text"},{"code":"Equatable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":": two correct trees can choose different parents on ties.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"metadata":{"role":"symbol","remoteSource":{"url":"https:\/\/github.com\/Tyler-Keith-Thompson\/Grafluent\/blob\/main\/Sources\/ShortestPaths\/ShortestPathTree.swift#L26","fileName":"ShortestPathTree.swift"},"title":"ShortestPathTree","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"ShortestPathTree"}],"modules":[{"name":"ShortestPaths"}],"roleHeading